{{draft}} {{Infobox deity|image=File:Udamikumari Shr Kami.jpg|script=水分神|name=Mikumari-no-Kami|script_name=水分神}}Ame-no-mikumari-no-kami is a Japanese [[kami]] she is worshipped at {{ill|Uda Mikumari Shrine|en}} ‎and {{ill|Yoshino Mikumari Shrine|en}} ‎<ref>https://archive.ph/wip/1ixNW</ref>. Due to a pun she is associated with pregnancy and childbirth.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2015-09-03 |title=Yoshino Mikumari Shrine - Nara |url=https://en.japantravel.com/nara/yoshino-mikumari-shrine/22951 |access-date=2023-10-17 |website=JapanTravel |language=en}}</ref> [[en:draft:Ame-no-mikumari-no-kami]] The most famous '''mikumari''' shrine is that of Yoshino<ref name="Grapard-1982">{{Cite journal |last=Grapard |first=Allan G. |date=1982 |title=Flying Mountains and Walkers of Emptiness: Toward a Definition of Sacred Space in Japanese Religions |url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/1062158 |journal=History of Religions |volume=21 |issue=3 |pages=195–221 |issn=0018-2710}}</ref>. Such shrines are frequently found in mountains and linked to Buddhism through {{ill|Shinbutsu-shūgō|en}}.<ref name="Grapard-1982" /> The etymology means water distribution.<ref name="Grapard-1982" />
