The most famous '''mikumari''' shrine is that of Yoshino<ref name="Grapard-1982">{{Cite journal |last=Grapard |first=Allan G. |date=1982 |title=Flying Mountains and Walkers of Emptiness: Toward a Definition of Sacred Space in Japanese Religions |url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/1062158 |journal=History of Religions |volume=21 |issue=3 |pages=195–221 |issn=0018-2710}}</ref>. Such shrines are frequently found in mountains and linked to Buddhism through {{ill|Shinbutsu-shūgō|en}}.<ref name="Grapard-1982" /> The etymology means [[water]] [[distribution]].<ref name="Grapard-1982" /> As tutelary deity of water management, Mikumari is specially honored, frequently linked to the origins of agricultural irrigation systems. She is seen as a guardian watching over fertility and facilitating childbirth, as opposed to {{ill|Suijin|en}} who is more about water in general, including water safety and sewage management.<ref name=":1">{{Cite web|title=Suijin, Water Divinity of Japan, Shinto Origin, Patron of Fishermen, Fertility, Motherhood|url=https://www.onmarkproductions.com/html/suijin.html|access-date=2024-04-02|website=www.onmarkproductions.com}}</ref>

