'''Mikumari-no-kami''' (水分神) is a Japanese [[kami]] she is worshipped at {{ill|Uda Mikumari Shrine|en}} ‎and {{ill|Yoshino Mikumari Shrine|en}} ‎<ref name=":0">https://archive.ph/wip/1ixNW</ref>. Due to a pun she is associated with [[pregnancy]] and [[childbirth]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=2015-09-03 |title=Yoshino Mikumari Shrine - Nara |url=https://en.japantravel.com/nara/yoshino-mikumari-shrine/22951 |access-date=2023-10-17 |website=JapanTravel |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Mikumari Shrine {{!}} Things to Do in Yoshino {{!}} All Japan Tours|url=https://alljapantours.com/japan-attraction/shrines/kansai/mikumari-shrine/|access-date=2024-03-25|website=alljapantours.com}}</ref>

The most famous '''mikumari''' shrine is that of Yoshino<ref name="Grapard-1982">{{Cite journal |last=Grapard |first=Allan G. |date=1982 |title=Flying Mountains and Walkers of Emptiness: Toward a Definition of Sacred Space in Japanese Religions |url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/1062158 |journal=History of Religions |volume=21 |issue=3 |pages=195–221 |issn=0018-2710}}</ref>. Such shrines are frequently found in mountains and linked to Buddhism through {{ill|Shinbutsu-shūgō|en|4=fr|5=Shinbutsu shūgō}}.<ref name="Grapard-1982" /> The etymology means [[water]] [[distribution]].<ref name="Grapard-1982" /> As tutelary deity of water management, Mikumari is specially honored, frequently linked to the origins of agricultural irrigation systems. She is seen as a guardian watching over fertility and facilitating childbirth, as opposed to {{ill|Suijin|en|4=fr|6=ja|7=水神}} who is more about water in general, including water safety and sewage management.<ref name=":1">{{Cite web|title=Suijin, Water Divinity of Japan, Shinto Origin, Patron of Fishermen, Fertility, Motherhood|url=https://www.onmarkproductions.com/html/suijin.html|access-date=2024-04-02|website=www.onmarkproductions.com}}</ref>

In Japanese mythology, it is said that Ame-no-Mikumari-no-Kami (God of Heavenly Water Distribution) and Kuni-no-Mikumari-no-Kami (God of Earthly Water Distribution) were the children of {{ill|Hayaakitsuhiko and Hayaakitsuhime|lt=Hayaakitsuhiko and Hayaakitsuhime|ja|ハヤアキツヒコ・ハヤアキツヒメ|en:draft|6=simple|7=User:Immanuelle/Hayaakitsuhiko and Hayaakitsuhime|8=fr|9=User:Immanuelle/Hayaakitsuhiko and Hayaakitsuhime}} .<ref name=":0" />

Suijin, in Japanese Shintoism, refers to the numerous celestial and terrestrial manifestations of the benevolent water deity, encompassing a wide range of mythological creatures related to water. Mikumari, as the "kami of water distribution", is revered in this specific context, often associated with the sources of agricultural waterways and considered protective in the areas of fertility and easy childbirth. While Suijin is honored at a multitude of shrines across Japan for its protection over fishermen and its patronage of fertility, Mikumari is specifically invoked for the distribution and management of water, a vital aspect for agriculture.<ref name=":1" /> 
[[en:draft:Ame-no-mikumari-no-kami]]
