==Overview==

[[Image:Descendants of Izanagi and Izanami.svg|thumb|right|250px|Kami-umi mythology (神産み) showing the gods born of Izanagi and Izanami. ]]

'''Mikumari no Kami''' (水分神) is a {{ill|Shinto|ja|神道|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Shinto|qq=}} deity associated with the control and distribution of {{ill|water|ja|水|1=Water (wuxing)|lt=Water (水)|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/water|qq=}}. The name "Kumari" derives from the term "kubari" (配り), meaning "distribution," and these deities are often enshrined at water sources and distribution points such as springs and river junctions.

In {{ill|Japanese mythology|ja|日本神話|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Japanese mythology|qq=}}, during the {{ill|Kami-umi|ja|神産み|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kami-umi|qq=}} (birth of gods) segment, the deities '''Ame no Mikumari no Kami''' (天水分神) and '''Kuni no Mikumari no Kami''' (国水分神) are described as the offspring of {{ill|Hayakitsuhiko and Hayakitsuhime|lt=Hayaakitsuhiko|ja|ハヤアキツヒコ|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Hayakitsuhiko and Hayakitsuhime|qq=}} and {{ill|Hayakitsuhiko and Hayakitsuhime|lt=Hayaakitsuhime|ja|ハヤアキツヒメ|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Hayakitsuhiko and Hayakitsuhime|qq=}}.

As water-related deities, they are often worshipped for {{ill|rainmaking|ja|祈雨|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/rainmaking|qq=}} and associated with {{ill|Ta-no-Kami|lt=rice field deities|ja|田の神|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Ta-no-Kami|qq=}} or, when enshrined at water sources, with {{ill|mountain deities|ja|山の神|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/mountain deities|qq=}}. Later, the term "Mikumari" was interpreted as "Mikomori" (御子守), leading to their veneration as guardians of {{ill|children|ja|子供|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/children|qq=}} and as deities of childbearing and safe childbirth.

